course: dictionary
lesson: 6153
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. A millstone is a pair of large, round, flat stones used in a mill. «A water-mill uses moving water to turn a wheel, which turns heavy millstones that grind grain into flour.» The clues are mill, grain and flour, so this is the real stone.
explain: The second meaning is a picture. A millstone round your neck is a heavy problem or responsibility you cannot get rid of. Nobody has a stone tied to them. The words round your neck show it is a figure of speech.
explain: To tell which meaning is meant, ask what the sentence is about. If it talks about grinding, mills or bread, use the first meaning. If it talks about a problem that weighs someone down, use the second. Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A millstone is a pair of large, round, flat stones used in a mill.
fact: Millstones are turned to grind grain into flour.
fact: In a water-mill, moving water turns a wheel, and the wheel drives gears that turn the millstones.
fact: In 1086 the Domesday Book counted more than 5,000 mills in England.
fact: A millstone round your neck means a heavy problem or responsibility that you cannot get rid of.
exercise: «The water wheel turned the millstones all day to grind the grain.» What were the millstones used for? | Carrying water up the hill | Keeping boats afloat on the river | Building the walls of a castle | *Grinding grain into flour
exercise: Dad says, «That debt is a millstone round my neck.» What does millstone mean here? | Two large, round, flat stones in a mill | *A heavy problem he cannot get rid of | A stone to skip across a pond | A sweet, chewy treat
exercise: «A water-mill uses moving water to turn a wheel.» What powers the water-mill? | Wind blowing through sails | Electric motors | Horses pulling a cart | *Moving water
test: «The old mill had two heavy millstones that ground the wheat.» Which meaning of millstone fits? | A heavy problem to carry | *A large, round, flat stone used to grind grain | A wheel on a cart | A kind of bread
test: «Owing so much money was a millstone round his neck for years.» What does this sentence suggest? | A fine present he was given | A special mill that makes flour very quickly | A necklace made of polished stone | *A heavy burden that was very hard to shake off
exam: Jayden asks, «How can I tell which meaning of millstone a writer means?» What is the best answer? | Pick the first meaning every time | Guess and move on without checking | *Read the words around it and check which meaning fits | Look only at the title
